# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Nahum'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'prophets'
SUBTITLE = 'The Burden of Nineveh, and the LORD Who Knows Those Who Trust Him'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Nahum is “the burden of Nineveh. The book of the vision of Nahum the Elkoshite” (Nah. 1:1). It describes the LORD slow to anger and great in power, His way in the whirlwind, His goodness to those who trust in Him, the fall of Nineveh, and good tidings for Judah (Nah. 1:3, 7, 15; 3:7, 19).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where the LORD speaks of Nineveh’s fall and of Judah and Jacob restored (Nah. 1:15; 2:2). Its words on the LORD’s character are also placed in the Universal Truths Table.')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Nahum', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — The LORD’s Character and Judgment (Ch. 1)'), ('p', 'The LORD is slow to anger and good to those who trust Him; good tidings come to Judah (Nah. 1:3, 7, 15).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Siege of Nineveh (Ch. 2)'), ('p', 'The LORD turns away the excellency of Jacob, and Nineveh is emptied (Nah. 2:2).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Woe to the Bloody City (Ch. 3)'), ('p', 'Nineveh is laid waste, and her wound has no healing (Nah. 3:7, 19).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Nahum', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>A Burden Against Nineveh</b> — “The burden of Nineveh” (Nah. 1:1).', '<b>Slow to Anger</b> — “The LORD is slow to anger, and great in power, and will not at all acquit the wicked” (Nah. 1:3).', '<b>A Strong Hold</b> — “The LORD is good, a strong hold in the day of trouble; and he knoweth them that trust in him” (Nah. 1:7).', '<b>Good Tidings for Judah</b> — “Behold upon the mountains the feet of him that bringeth good tidings, that publisheth peace!” (Nah. 1:15).', '<b>The Excellency of Jacob</b> — “The LORD hath turned away the excellency of Jacob, as the excellency of Israel” (Nah. 2:2).', '<b>No Healing</b> — “There is no healing of thy bruise; thy wound is grievous” (Nah. 3:19).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Nahum', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Nahum points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Bearer of Good Tidings</b> — The feet of him that publisheth peace (Nah. 1:15) are reported as written; Isaiah 52:7 has similar words and is read in its own chapter.', '<b>The LORD a Strong Hold</b> — The LORD is “a strong hold in the day of trouble” (Nah. 1:7).', '<b>Judgment Without Acquittal</b> — The LORD “will not at all acquit the wicked” (Nah. 1:3).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Nahum', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Nahum speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Nahum operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Nahum in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Nahum reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['Judah called to keep her solemn feasts and perform her vows (Nah. 1:15)', 'Jacob and Israel, whose excellency the LORD turns away and restores (Nah. 2:2)', 'the LORD’s judgment on Nineveh for the sake of His people (Nah. 1:1, 15)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Nahum that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Nahum contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Burden of Nineveh — “The burden of Nineveh.” (Nah. 1:1).', 'Good Tidings to Judah — “O Judah, keep thy solemn feasts, perform thy vows.” (Nah. 1:15).', 'The Excellency of Jacob — “the LORD hath turned away the excellency of Jacob, as the excellency of Israel.” (Nah. 2:2).', 'Nineveh Laid Waste — “Nineveh is laid waste: who will bemoan her?” (Nah. 3:7).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Nahum Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Nahum and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Nahum teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Nahum', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Burden of Nineveh', '“The burden of Nineveh.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Nah. 1:1'], ['Good Tidings to Judah', '“O Judah, keep thy solemn feasts, perform thy vows.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Nah. 1:15'], ['The Excellency of Jacob', '“the LORD hath turned away the excellency of Jacob, as the excellency of Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Nah. 2:2'], ['Nineveh Laid Waste', '“Nineveh is laid waste: who will bemoan her?”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Nah. 3:7']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Nahum that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Nahum teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Slow to Anger', '“The LORD is slow to anger, and great in power, and will not at all acquit the wicked.”', 'Nah. 1:3'], ['A Strong Hold', '“The LORD is good, a strong hold in the day of trouble.”', 'Nah. 1:7'], ['Knows Those Who Trust', '“he knoweth them that trust in him.”', 'Nah. 1:7'], ['Wickedness Judged', '“upon whom hath not thy wickedness passed continually?”', 'Nah. 3:19']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Nahum without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Nahum in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Trust Him in trouble</b> — He is a strong hold (Nah. 1:7).', '<b>Know that He knows</b> — He knoweth them that trust (Nah. 1:7).', '<b>Remember His patience and justice</b> — Slow to anger, and He will not acquit the wicked (Nah. 1:3).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Nahum', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Nahum in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['The LORD’s Character (Ch. 1) — Slow to anger, a strong hold. <i>(Nah. 1:3, 7, 15)</i>', 'The Siege of Nineveh (Ch. 2) — Jacob’s excellency. <i>(Nah. 2:2)</i>', 'Woe to the Bloody City (Ch. 3) — Nineveh laid waste. <i>(Nah. 3:7, 19)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Nahum, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward trust in the LORD, patience with His justice, and good tidings of peace without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Nahum simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Nahum stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]

# Place in God's Storyline (Master Standard v1.155): last item of Section 1
